    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec1-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from the article outlines the mechanisms by which epitranscriptomic modifications impact host gene expression and their significance in regulating cellular processes relevant to Theileria annulata infection.\n\nThey modify host gene expression through both post-transcriptional and post-translational mechanisms to support their development [6] . Epigenetic regulation, which encompasses histone modifications, DNA modifications, and non-coding RNAs, plays pivotal roles in gene expression control [7] — influencing cell homeostasis, differentiation, and development [8] . Additionally, an emerging epigenetic perspective suggests similar chemical modification processes on both mRNA and other RNA types [9] . While epigenetic alterations of DNA and chromatin are studied across a spectrum of organisms, including parasitic protists and mammals, the 'epitranscriptome,' notably reversible RNA modifications [10] , remains relatively unexplored except in select mammalian species and some model organisms [8] .",
      "schema:name": "Introduction:",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Theileria annulata",
          "beacon:entityType": "ORGANISM"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec1-p2",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from Malak Haidar's 2025 article discusses the limited exploration of the 'epitranscriptome,' particularly focusing on reversible RNA modifications like N 6 -methyladenosine (m 6 A) in various organisms, including parasitic protists.\n\nWhile epigenetic alterations of DNA and chromatin are studied across a spectrum of organisms, including parasitic protists and mammals, the 'epitranscriptome,' notably reversible RNA modifications [10] , remains relatively unexplored except in select mammalian species and some model organisms [8] . N 6 -methyladenosine (m 6 A) stands out as a predominant, reversible modification found on eukaryotic mRNA [11] . The relative abundance of m 6 A in mRNA transcripts impacts RNA processes such as splicing [12] , nuclear export [13] , RNA degradation [14] and translation [15] . Furthermore, m 6 A plays a crucial role in several biological operations, including the DNA damage response [16] , determination of cell fate [17] , innate immunity [18] and tumorigenesis , as reviewed in [19] .",
      "schema:name": "Introduction:",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "N 6 -methyladenosine",
          "beacon:entityType": "CHEMICAL",
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_17196"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
