        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "DNA damage response",
          "beacon:entityType": "BIOLOGICAL_PROCESS"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "cell fate determination",
          "beacon:entityType": "BIOLOGICAL_PROCESS"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "innate immunity",
          "beacon:entityType": "BIOLOGICAL_PROCESS"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "tumorigenesis",
          "beacon:entityType": "BIOLOGICAL_PROCESS"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "m6A",
          "beacon:predicate": "regulates",
          "beacon:object": "DNA damage response",
          "beacon:evidence": "m6A plays a crucial role in several biological operations, including the DNA damage response"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec1-p4",
      "beacon:sectionType": "introduction",
      "schema:text": "This introduction section from a study on Theileria annulata discusses the role of m6A methyltransferase complexes, highlighting the functions of METTL3, METTL14, and WTAP in m6A mRNA modifications relevant to host-parasite interactions.\n\nIntriguingly, m 6 A peaks can aggregate in distinct regions, serving various biological functions [25] . m 6 A methyltransferase complexes oversee all m 6 A mRNA modifications. Central to the complex is a heterodimer comprising two writer-proteins: METTL3 and METTL14. METTL3 is the catalytic core, while METTL14 provides structural support. WTAP (Wilms' Tumor 1-Associating Protein) stabilizes this core. RNA-Binding Motif protein 15 (RBM15) assists in guiding the complex to its targets. Conversely, \"erasers\" such as the fat mass and obesity-associated protein (FTO) and alkB homolog 5 (ALKBH5) function as demethylases, removing m 6 A marks. Several m6A-binding proteins, including YTHDF1–3 and YTHDC1, are identified as m 6 A \"readers\" [26-29] . YTH domain family proteins might interact with unique subsets of m6A sites, leading to diverse gene expression outcomes [30] .",
      "schema:name": "Introduction:",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "METTL3",
          "beacon:entityType": "PROTEIN",
          "@id": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gene/56339"
        },
        {
