    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec2-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "methods",
      "schema:text": "This Materials and Methods section from Malak Haidar's 2025 study details the pharmacological inhibition of HIF-1α in Theileria-infected cells and outlines the procedures for cell counting and ATP assay to ensure consistent cell concentration.\n\nFor pharmacological inhibition of HIF-1α, Theileria -infected cells were treated with 100 μM of PX478 (MedKoo Biosciences) for up to 16 h. Cell Counting and ATP Assay: To count the number of cells, we employed the TC20 automated cell counter from BioRad. We ensured the cell concentration remained consistent at approximately 1 to 2 × 10ˆ5 cells/mL. The TC20 cell counter quickly provides a comprehensive cell count (with or without the aid of trypan blue staining) and evaluates cell viability using the trypan blue exclusion method. For assessing cell viability, we utilized the ATPlite Luminescence Assay (PerkinElmer). This assay offers a uniform method to determine the number of viable cells in a culture by measuring the ATP content, indicative of metabolically active cells. We conducted all experiments three times independently, each in duplicate. Error bars depict the standard error of the mean (SEM) across the three biological replicates.",
      "schema:name": "Materials and Methods",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "HIF-1α",
          "beacon:entityType": "PROTEIN",
