    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p1",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This Results section from the study by Malak Haidar explores how T. annulata infection influences mRNA methylation in bovine leukocytes, highlighting a gap in understanding the parasite’s impact on host transcriptomes.\n\nWhile we, along with others, have pinpointed components of the disrupted transcriptomes in Theileria -infected macrophages and B-cells [40, 41] , the capacity of the parasite to alter the mRNA methylome of its transformed host leukocyte remains unexplored. To gain a comprehensive insight into m 6 A mRNA methylation patterns across various T. annulata -infected cell lines, we used the ELISA assay to measure m 6 A RNA methylation levels. We extracted total RNA from virulent (V) and attenuated (A) macrophages, merozoite-producing macrophages (Vm), and infected B-cells (TBL20), as well as their non-infected counterparts (BL20) for this assay. Intriguingly, merozoite-producing macrophages (Vm and Am) exhibited higher m6A percentages than both virulent and attenuated macrophages ( Figure. 1 Theileria -infected B cells (TBL20) had a reduced m 6 A percentage compared to their uninfected BL20 counterparts ( Figure. 1 A, right).",
      "schema:name": "Results:",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "T. annulata infection alters bovine mRNA methylation in host leukocytes",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "T. annulata",
          "beacon:entityType": "ORGANISM"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "mRNA",
          "beacon:entityType": "GENE",
          "@id": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gene/118229251"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "m 6 A",
          "beacon:entityType": "CHEMICAL",
