        }
      ],
      "beacon:figureRefs": [
        "fig1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p2",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This Results section from the study explores how T. annulata infection impacts m6A methylation patterns in bovine host leukocytes, utilizing m6A-seq analyses to assess changes in mRNA expression levels.\n\n1 A, right). To further understand the role of m6A methylation as an epigenetic marker in T. annulata -infected leukocytes, we conducted transcriptome-wide m6A-seq (MeRIP-seq) analyses. This method detects methylated regions as peaks in transcript coverage by comparing immunoprecipitated RNA to input RNA. Our assessment of the influence of m6A methylation' on mRNA expression found no difference in expression levels between methylated and non-methylated mRNAs ( Figure.1B ). Notably, while ELISA assays showed increased m6A percentages in merozoite-producing macrophages (Vm) compared to virulent (V) and attenuated (A) macrophages, the MeRIP-seq analyses indicated higher m6A peaks in proliferating V and A macrophages than in proliferation arresting Vm. However, this high level is not statistically significant. It's important to highlight that ELISA offers a bulk measurement of m6A modification, representing an average m6A level across an entire RNA sample. In comparison, MeRIP-seq delivers a detailed, transcriptome-wide view of m6A sites, ensuring precise detection and quantification of m6A at specific sites throughout the transcriptome. This distinction could account for the observed discrepancy in our results. However, both techniques identified clear differences in m6A levels between proliferating V/A macrophages and proliferation-arresting Vm. For further validation, we analyzed the expression profiles of methyltransferases, writers, demethylases, and readers, specifically mettl3 , wtap , alkbh5 , ythdc1 , ythdf1 , and ythdf3 . This was executed between BL20 and TBL20 and solely in virulent versus Vm, as there was no discernible difference in m6A percentages between virulent and attenuated macrophages. Remarkably, mRNA expression levels of methyltransferases, demethylases, and readers were elevated in merozoites relative to virulent macrophages ( Figure 2 Theileria -infected B cells (TBL20) when compared to the non-infected BL20 ( Figure. 2 B).",
      "schema:name": "Results:",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "T. annulata infection alters bovine mRNA methylation in host leukocytes",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "T. annulata",
          "beacon:entityType": "ORGANISM"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "m6A",
          "beacon:entityType": "CHEMICAL",
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_21891"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "mRNA",
          "beacon:entityType": "GENE",
          "@id": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gene/118229251"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Vm",
          "beacon:entityType": "CELL_LINE",
          "@id": "https://www.cellosaurus.org/CVCL_0B29"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "T. annulata",
