    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p3",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This Results section from the study investigates the relationship between m6A methylation and differential gene expression, revealing that 232 out of 4161 evaluated genes display significant differential expression in Theileria annulata-infected leukocytes.\n\n2 B). ### Association Between m6A Methylation and Differential Gene Expression\n\nTo explore the potential relationships between m6A methylation and differential gene expression (DE), our analysis began by comparing different gene sets. In the V vs. A comparison, of the 4161 genes evaluated, 232 were found to be differentially expressed in either direction, representing approximately 10.8% of the total. However, when narrowing our focus to just those genes with m6A marks, 314 out of the 2785 genes showed differential expression, translating to approximately 11.3%. Utilizing a binomial distribution test to ascertain the probability of observing 314 DE genes out of 2785 (given a baseline DE probability of 0.108 derived from the entire gene set), we arrived at a p-value of 0.2252. This result indicates that the frequency of differential expression in genes with m6A modifications does not significantly deviate from what would be expected based on the overall gene set.",
      "schema:name": "Results:",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Association Between m6A Methylation and Differential Gene Expression",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "m6A",
          "beacon:entityType": "CHEMICAL",
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_21891"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Theileria annulata",
          "beacon:entityType": "ORGANISM"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "leukocytes",
          "beacon:entityType": "CELL_LINE",
          "@id": "https://www.cellosaurus.org/CVCL_M029"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "genes",
          "beacon:entityType": "GENE"
        }
      ],
      "beacon:relations": [
        {
          "@type": "beacon:Relation",
          "beacon:subject": "m6A",
          "beacon:predicate": "associated_with",
          "beacon:object": "differential gene expression",
          "beacon:evidence": "the relationship between m6A methylation and differential gene expression"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p4",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This results section from Malak Haidar's study discusses the association of m6A methylation with differential gene expression in Theileria annulata-infected leukocytes, highlighting significant findings from specific gene comparisons.\n\nThis result indicates that the frequency of differential expression in genes with m6A modifications does not significantly deviate from what would be expected based on the overall gene set. In stark contrast, the comparison of V vs. Vm and V vs. A returned p-values of 0.0222 and 0.00414, respectively. These Figures suggest that, in these comparisons, genes exhibiting m6A peaks are differentially expressed more often than one would expect by chance or based on the probabilities inherent to the entire gene set ( Figure 3",
