    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p5",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This results section from the study explores the association between m6A methylation and differential gene expression in Theileria annulata-infected leukocytes, highlighting a correlation between m6A peaks and gene up-regulation.\n\nThese Figures suggest that, in these comparisons, genes exhibiting m6A peaks are differentially expressed more often than one would expect by chance or based on the probabilities inherent to the entire gene set ( Figure 3 Diving deeper, we sought to uncover any potential links between the presence of m6A in one sample and the up-regulation of genes in another, guided by the notion that methylation might suppress mRNA abundance. For the V vs A comparison, 218 out of the 4161 genes were found to be up-regulated in the A sample, which corresponds to roughly 5.24%. Among the 531 genes that were exclusively methylated in V, 29 (or about 5.46%) were up-regulated in A. The probability of witnessing 29 DE genes (those up-regulated specifically in V) out of 531, with an inherent up-regulation chance of 5.24%, yields a p-value of 0.436, suggesting no significant association. An analogous test, focusing on genes uniquely methylated in A that were up-regulated in V, produced a p-value of 0.744. The V vs Vm comparison ( Figure. 3 A, middle panel) revealed no significant enrichment for genes uniquely marked with m6A in V that were up-regulated in Vm (p-value: 0.513). However, the reverse comparison, Vm vs V, highlighted an enrichment for genes marked exclusively with m6A in Vm that were up-regulated in V (p-value: 0.0163). Moreover, a marked enrichment was observed for genes uniquely bearing m6A marks in Vm and up-regulated in A (p-value: 0.0382) ( Figure. 3 A, bottom panel).",
      "schema:name": "Results:",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "Association Between m6A Methylation and Differential Gene Expression",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
