    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p9",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This Results section from the study on Theileria annulata details how m6A modification promotes the proliferation of infected leukocytes, highlighting the impact of T. annulata on host B cell dynamics.\n\nIt's paramount to note that this is a broad observation, and individual genes might exhibit distinct behaviors. ### m6A modification enhances the proliferation of Theileria -infected leukocytes\n\nTheileria can transform host leukocytes and induce their uncontrolled proliferation. Infection of BL20 B cells by T. annulata to give TBL20 increases B cell proliferation, whereas when T. annulata -infected macrophages (V) are induced to produce merozoites macrophages (Vm) proliferation arrests [42] . Observing that m6A levels rise in the more proliferative TBL20 B cells and decline in the arrested Vm macrophages, we postulated a correlation between m 6 A levels and host cell proliferative activity.",
      "schema:name": "Results:",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "m6A modification enhances the proliferation of Theileria -infected leukocytes",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Theileria annulata",
          "beacon:entityType": "ORGANISM"
        },
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
