    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p12",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This Results section from Malak Haidar's study highlights the role of m6A modification in enhancing the proliferation of Theileria-infected leukocytes, focusing on specific bovine mRNAs related to cell cycle regulation.\n\nS1)). Acknowledging that host cell proliferation is pivotal in Theileria -mediated pathogenesis [44] , we sifted through our Me-RIP dataset to identify m6A-modified bovine mRNAs transcribed by genes integral to G2 and G2/M phase modulation such as CDC2, CDK1, CCNB1, CCNB2, BUB1, BUB1B, and CDC25B,Cyclin B1,E2F4 [45, 46] . Among these, only the E2F transcription factor family member (E2F4) bore m6A modifications. Earlier research affirmed that E2F4 partly functions as a transcriptional catalyst, regulating the expression of cell cycle-associated genes [47] . Notably, our meRIP-seq dataset revealed heightened levels of methylated E2F4 mRNA relative to its non-methylated counterpart. To validate the hypothesis that m6A modification modulates E2F4 expression, we quantified E2F4 mRNA levels post METTL3 overexpression in virulent macrophages. E2F4 expression heightens in conjunction with rising m6A levels ( Figure.4C , right). For more confirmation, we looked for genes known to be regulated by E2F4 in bovine cells [48, 49] , but we could only identify four genes: SIRT5, mTOR, SREBP‐1c (gene is SREBF1), and Cyclin D1 (gene is CCND1). We indicated SIRT5, mTOR, and SREBP‐1c on a volcano plot (Fig. S2). None of these genes show significant differential gene expression (DGE), but they are all downregulated in Vm compared to V. We then examined a ChIP-Seq study from humans [50] . This study identified 16,246 peaks (putative E2F4 binding sites) in the hg18 genome. Using this annotation, we found that 4,739 human genes have peaks within their 250 bp upstream region. Of these, we identified 3,960 corresponding bovine genes in our RNA DEseq output. Upon examining these bovine genes, where the human version has an upstream putative E2F4 binding site, we observed that 43 are upregulated and 114 are downregulated. Over 72% of these DGE genes are downregulated (corresponding to the repressor activity of E2F4) compared to the rest of the DGE genes, of which only approximately 35% are downregulated. This finding is highly significant (attached, bottom plot) and suggests that genes potentially regulated by E2F4 are downregulated in Vm (Fig. S2). Collectively, these findings reinforce the notion that m6A modulates cell cycle dynamics via E2F4 mRNA modifications.",
      "schema:name": "Results:",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "m6A modification enhances the proliferation of Theileria -infected leukocytes",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Theileria",
