    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p13",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This Results section from the study by Malak Haidar discusses how HIF-1α influences the proliferation of Theileria-infected leukocytes through m6A-RNA methylation, highlighting higher levels in attenuated macrophages compared to virulent forms.\n\nCollectively, these findings reinforce the notion that m6A modulates cell cycle dynamics via E2F4 mRNA modifications. ### HIF-1α-promotes proliferation of Theileria -infected leukocytes by inducing m6A-RNA methylation\n\nHIF-1α levels are significantly higher in attenuated macrophages than in their virulent counterparts, even after losing their tumorigenic phenotype [51] . Pharmacological inhibition of HIF-1α is found to prevent the proliferation of Theileria -infected macrophages [51] . Additionally, HIF-1α inhibition in T. annulata -infected B cells abolishes their ability to form colonies in soft agar, most likely due to growth arrest [52] . Importantly, HIF-1α has been shown to enhance the expression of WTAP leading to a change in m 6 A distribution and boosting cell proliferation in acute myeloid leukemia [53] . HIF-1α also directly binds to the promoter region of YTHDF1, regulating its transcription under hypoxic conditions in hepatocellular carcinoma [54] .",
      "schema:name": "Results:",
      "beacon:subsectionTitle": "HIF-1α-promotes proliferation of Theileria -infected leukocytes by inducing m6A-RNA methylation",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
