    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec3-p15",
      "beacon:sectionType": "results",
      "schema:text": "This Results section from Malak Haidar's study examines the role of HIF-1α in promoting the proliferation of Theileria-infected leukocytes through m6A-RNA methylation, highlighting protein expression differences and experimental methods to analyze methylation levels.\n\nWe observed increased HIF-1α protein expression in Vm compared to virulent macrophages ( Figure 5 A, left). Subsequently, we used PX-478 to inhibit the HIF-1α signaling pathway in virulent macrophages and infected B cells (TBL20) and study its effect on the m 6 A RNA methylation state. The values of percent m6A in each sample were determined by ELISA assay and mRNA expression levels of writers and readers of the m 6 A complex were tested by qPCR. When HIF-1α is suppressed, the m 6 A percentage shown in infected macrophages as well as TBL20 is found to be upregulated. ( Figure 5 A, right) Furthermore, this increase is in line with the WTAP, YTDHF1, and YTHDF3 up-regulation in both virulent macrophages and infected- BL20 ( Figure. 6 6 A complex expression and thereby drives proliferative effects in Theileria -infected cells.",
