    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec4-p2",
      "beacon:sectionType": "discussion",
      "schema:text": "This discussion section from the article examines the differences in m6A methylation levels between Theileria-infected B cells and uninfected cells, highlighting findings from MeRIP-seq analyses and the lack of correlation with mRNA expression levels.\n\nAdditionally, m6A levels differ between the more proliferative Theileria -infected B cells (TBL20) and their uninfected counterparts, BL20. In tandem with these findings, MeRIP-seq analyses shed light on detailed m6A methylation patterns across the transcriptome. While m6A peaks within genes displayed consistency, there was no clear correlation with mRNA expression levels. Interestingly, ELISA indicated a rise in m6A levels in merozoite-producing macrophages, a trend contradicted by the MeRIP-seq analysis. This disparity accentuates the distinct strengths of the two techniques: the overarching perspective of ELISA versus the nuanced detail of MeRIP-seq. These differences underscore the importance of employing a range of methodologies in epigenetics research to achieve a comprehensive understanding.",
      "schema:name": "Discussion:",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "Theileria",
