    {
      "@type": "schema:WebPageElement",
      "beacon:chunkId": "article-10-1016-j-bj-2025-100828-sec4-p6",
      "beacon:sectionType": "discussion",
      "schema:text": "This discussion section from the article examines the role of m6A methylation in downregulating gene expression and its significance in modulating host cell proliferation during T. annulata infection.\n\nDelving deeper, some genes showed exclusive methylation in specific categories, pointing towards a potential role for m6A in downregulation of gene expression. Furthermore, this research emphasizes the role of m6A methylation in modulating host cell proliferation during T. annulata infection. Often paralleled with DNA methylation regarding its regulatory significance, m6A might be a key factor driving the persistent proliferation of T. annulata -infected leukocytes. The observed reduction in proliferation in METTL3-overexpressing T. annulata -infected leukocytes accentuates the critical role of m6A in this setting. It also raises questions regarding the potential regulatory influence of m6A on E2F4 expression, a crucial component of the cell cycle, suggesting a layered control mechanism.",
      "schema:name": "Discussion:",
      "schema:mentions": [
        {
          "@type": "schema:Thing",
          "schema:name": "m6A",
          "beacon:entityType": "CHEMICAL",
          "@id": "http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_21891"
        },
